d by a sports magazine on a random sample of $125$ students from various U.S. universities reported that $40$ respondents love soccer. Find a $99%$ confidence interval for the national proportion of college students who love soccer.\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0, 1)$, the table below shows the $z$-scores $z_{p}$ such that $P(Z > z_{p}) = p$ for some particular values of $p$.*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $z_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172338 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4a59a953-fd66-5704-998f-ee025eba0b9a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172338 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172722 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172723 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172722 #edb/ref 1061 176376 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172722 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "89f797af-a45c-58a5-8bec-5490a8e51df2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172722 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173212 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172723 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172724 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172723 #edb/ref 1061 176377 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172723 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "413a1302-d708-5a68-8969-eee6f8bf3793" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172723 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172724 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172725 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172724 #edb/ref 1061 176378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172724 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d86369dd-0073-526c-baf8-4f7a189e8246" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172724 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186173213 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172725 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186172726 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172725 #edb/ref 1061 176379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172725 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2700b310-2c62-5ffd-ba65-f8b258d201e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172725 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172726 #edb/ref 1061 176382 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172726 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "822975a7-7bca-5cc6-9812-46e5051e550a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186172726 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186171936 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173212 #edb/ref 1064 "Let's now discuss how to construct a confidence interval for the proportion of the population whose members have a particular characteristic. \n\nAs a concrete example, let $p$ represent the proportion of the United States voting population that will vote for candidate $A$ in the next presidential election. \n\nSuppose we conduct a random sample of size $n=40$ from our population. After processing our results, we find that the proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}$ of sample members that plan to vote for candidate $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} = 0.3.\n$$\n\nThis is our *estimate* of the population proportion $p.$ \n\nOf course, the quantity that we're *really* interested in is $p,$ the proportion of the entire voting population that will vote for candidate $A.$ We want to get an idea of how accurate our estimate is. Therefore, we wish to construct a confidence interval of the form\n\n$$\n(\\widehat{\\,p}-E, \\: \\widehat{\\,p}+E),\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $E$ is some **margin of error.**\n\nLet's now discuss how to construct a $95\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173212 #edb/ref 1065 6254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173212 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b64af41f-7266-5dc3-9360-aa6c591384e4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173212 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173213 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have a random sample of size $n$ from a sufficiently large population with sample proportion $\\widehat{\\,p}.$ Let's also assume that the following conditions are satisfied:\n\n- $n \\widehat{\\,p} > 5$\n\n- $n (1-\\widehat{\\,p}) > 5$\n\nThen, for a given value $\\alpha$ between $0$ and $1,$ a $[100(1-\\alpha)]\\%$ confidence interval for the population proportion $p$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\left(\n\\widehat{\\,p} - z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}},\\:\n\n\\widehat{\\,p} + z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}\n\\right).\n$$\n\nThe endpoints of our confidence interval are the **confidence limits** and are given by\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\,p} \\pm z_{\\alpha/2} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}.\n$$\n\nEach part of the formula above has a name:\n\n- $\\!\\widehat{\\,p}$ is the sample proportion\n\n- $p$ is the population proportion\n\n- $n$ is the sample size\n\n- $z_{\\alpha/2}$ is the corresponding $z$-score\n\n- $E = z_{\\alpha/2}\\cdot  \\sqrt{\\dfrac{\\widehat{\\,p}(1-\\widehat{\\,p})}{n}}$ is the margin of error\n\n![](images/d0/d07c13f30dc4a0960b70f2705bb615f79c0c7ef3a6ed5d7a8eef2401db02d6c2.svg)\n\nSo, our confidence interval can be written as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\overset{^}{\\,p}-[\\text{margin of error}], & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+[\\text{margin of error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{^}{\\,p}-[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}], & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+[\\text{[math]z[/math]-score}]\\cdot [\\text{standard error}]) \\\\ (\\overset{^}{\\,p}-{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}}, & \\,\\overset{^}{\\,p}+{z}_{\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{\\overset{^}{\\,p}(1-\\overset{^}{\\,p})}{n}})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince computing a confidence interval amounts to finding the corresponding confidence limits, we will use the two terms interchangeably.\n\nFinally, in the future, you may assume that if a population is \"large,\" it is sufficiently larger than any sample drawn from it." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173213 #edb/ref 1065 6255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173213 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fe719d03-f27c-50c1-8c5d-9bf9e9326e10" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173213 #edb/ref 1067 "Confidence Intervals for Proportions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173223 #edb/ref 1064 "We have ![](images/4a/4a616e999005a19046f642d63f02ed9d7054edec758f6a03edb7eb90c29e8713.svg) and we wish to construct a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval for the population proportion ![](images/1d/1d3e45623fb292952d8c207690797505cc7c484a6410f4a95aff94a51b4a0ee5.svg)\n\nTo construct our confidence interval, the first thing to do is to establish the sampling distribution of ![](images/be/be3d0efacea6b66b85bae982c4d923f2473fe722d5f30c4b9a940ac67773cff2.svg)\n\nSince the United States has around ![](images/59/592c5e42c05234443b91e6d53afea4f21fb7e5dfb0ab1e645fce7ffe881bfc9c.svg) million registered voters, the sample size ![](images/35/3597ee702449dc99d1b986728db87df4b1e6ee3f2ff01bf7db08ad6c48a9bd42.svg) is clearly less than ![](images/41/414c7a992cb07b0cf60bef2446dc3eb4072aeb99acedf6d77c02b5c183cdfc63.svg) of the population size. This means that, provided certain conditions are met, the following approximation can be applied:\n\n![](images/24/24bc598b02901990c48811d40a22934c7f4d59be5e57bcb7f8ac6ca592d1abcb.svg)\n\nLet's check that our usual conditions hold in this case (where we use ![](images/0e/0e92bef16c8ef7c460a72dcba19c450d46eb09079ba887a47987d8af51df613f.svg) as an estimate for ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg)):\n\n![](images/bf/bfd75db155b00910cb00aa4c37df692c254df765ec94ba265af589f72868071d.svg)\n\nSince all three conditions hold, the above approximation is applicable. \n\nBy transforming ![](images/0e/0e92bef16c8ef7c460a72dcba19c450d46eb09079ba887a47987d8af51df613f.svg) to a standard normal random variable, we have\n\n![](images/c4/c461c3f9d172553d6893ddd8bb9f5f9490ba9b3135de8093a367d5286e306344.svg)\n\nSince the true population proportion ![](images/48/48c7b594e8628a17b991d4e65e7bee6ffe6680dd410f036cfae83154bbd55fc6.svg) is unknown, we substitute in this formula the sample proportion ![](images/0e/0e92bef16c8ef7c460a72dcba19c450d46eb09079ba887a47987d8af51df613f.svg) for ![](images/f5/f5d3e590cf0347a598e50f45f2e35ce219f3fd9589dfb69628aeb1ba0fdfa60c.svg)\n\n![](images/e7/e7867d67e36fcd33810b482ab26bdfe4be2da4549463e8f1602c65e5a3ae78a0.svg)\n\nWe wish to find a ![](images/90/9032cb23a878659e74b670d3779a8cd4793b28af921eab1a6b3f2041a815e03b.svg)-interval that we're ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confident that the random variable ![](images/2f/2ff8585a9cda3f4625f17d79f11a40484b5d11d3908b34b868581c0bfaf40db5.svg) lies within. This interval is indicated in the diagram below:\n\n![](images/50/50a0f49fe5c96ba3259832fbf1a0abcedffb342301ca069bb7b88d157a181019.png)\n\n  \n\nTo help with the notation, we define a new parameter ![](images/b2/b2382453bd24fb0facae9b8be51cbdb4f3606603ab108670fde4f7815fb5d7a4.svg) Since we're computing a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval, we have \n\n![](images/7e/7e6fa76dc615bf00f8cbdf957c653ba7b6217fda2dab0937b1e0cc0741cb7b61.svg)\n\nNotice that ![](images/28/28f62d4b860fec1ef5c6a7016529d78c308813779a7f12147e508bce162315a7.svg) is precisely the area bounded by each \"tail\" that we're excluding from our confidence interval. Let's label the critical values at the endpoints of our interval as ![](images/42/420634552d23ada1156e47b7a39985de21735a4d652674e15a481ef25cbf1c37.svg)\n\n![](images/3c/3cdfd2c72bf7fe65e5d1b584d46d4c846e74615e54c739a6d1ea990c95011a8a.png)\n\n  \n\nAccording to our diagram,\n\n![](images/48/4875e8608b48eb4fd92e20e26114baf19acb4257cc077d08581483181274d3cc.svg)  \n\nUsing a percentage points table for the standard normal distribution, we find that ![](images/74/7437fff22f0f020a39b79d5cb1470b545ba00b61f271a4ac9ee2778c7a549fe2.svg) Therefore,\n\n![](images/b9/b9ce27b3145e86201f04307c54d83681d016c11a60bd04a8159ca81b202c2bd1.svg)\n\nIn other words,\n\n![](images/c9/c9a4066e2b0ed64d8e64b524342b2c875a7bdc62f2eede2c4fb4a96354498ee4.svg)  \n\nTherefore, there is a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) probability that our random variable ![](images/2f/2ff8585a9cda3f4625f17d79f11a40484b5d11d3908b34b868581c0bfaf40db5.svg) will lie in the interval ![](images/b6/b62822fdda83ec83c86e736d28f8977e169d277b0a845bef6422a8feed2500d7.svg)\n\nNow, here's the trick. We solve the inequality inside the parentheses of the last probability statement for the population proportion ![](images/f5/f5d3e590cf0347a598e50f45f2e35ce219f3fd9589dfb69628aeb1ba0fdfa60c.svg)\n\n![](images/06/06861abae3aa843df157b835d2505f1e11b1b72108e482a492b98374a395cf14.svg)   \n\nTherefore, a ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval for the population proportion ![](images/03/0341b1d7529f66db72d7384047a23e81defd5fa012807825ced8393f6f7f9826.svg) is given by\n\n![](images/7e/7ea325842297236a6ed3c3704eb8fa3b6d3c4214d14fc6548c83a63e9d3e2f50.svg)\n\nFinally, substituting our values\n\n![](images/50/5044d5c3f2e8014d52f939897815813472d6010fe5b9f0903ca9e1fa2692420a.svg)\n\nwe obtain that our ![](images/ad/ad3179b1ff9f2cbef8f6af088f855b2d2b04b97f3e533621283cfdc90bc30ada.svg) confidence interval for ![](images/03/0341b1d7529f66db72d7384047a23e81defd5fa012807825ced8393f6f7f9826.svg) is \n\n![](images/95/95d52028e8b20a7ad509479ea958f31b698f3b74b8c24088ed68dd88bd3d0c90.svg) \n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n![](images/03/03e447d7a11205c8684e92a64b58d7db4e9f8de019c6f99af395211b46e42fdf.svg)\n\nNow that we've seen how to construct a particular confidence interval, let's discuss the more general procedure." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173223 #edb/ref 1065 6462 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173223 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "462ecbef-51e0-5a01-808e-cd71bd17cbc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186173223 #edb/ref 1067 "Constructing Confidence Intervals" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
